INT. NEONATAL ICU - NIGHT

Dim. Incubators glow like small aquariums. Monitors beep in patient intervals.

DR. AMARA OKELLO (40s, surgical mask pulled under her chin) checks a chart. Beside her, NURSE WOJTEK BIALEK (30s, soft-voiced) adjusts a tube.

She lifts the baby's tiny wrist. He holds a dosage syringe to the light. Both are quiet.

AMARA
Heart rate steady?

WOJTEK
Steady.

She nods. He adjusts the monitor. She smiles, a little.

AMARA
Go home, Wojtek.

WOJTEK
After my shift.

AMARA
It's three a.m.

WOJTEK
After my shift.

He moves to the next incubator. She follows. They check, they chart, they check again.

An alarm shrills. Both spin. The monitor on bed four flatlines.

WOJTEK (CONT'D)
Doctor!

AMARA
I see it.

She reaches in; he pulls the cover. She tilts the baby's chin, begins tiny compressions with two fingers. He hands her a syringe; she pushes it. Nothing. She compresses again.

WOJTEK (CONT'D)
Come on.

AMARA
Come on, little one.

A blip. Another. A rhythm. Both freeze, listening. The alarm cuts out.

She lets out a shaky breath. He sets the syringe down. Neither of them moves. She looks at him; he nods.

AMARA (CONT'D)
That was close.

WOJTEK
Too close.

He pulls a stool over and sits. She takes the chart off its hook and, without a word, starts writing.
